6.38.m:
Viṣṇu said:
“Whatever you say, blessed woman, all that shall come to pass;
you, auspicious one, shall grant all desires, and not otherwise.

The third, the eighth, the ninth, and the fourteenth lunar days,
but especially the eleventh—this lunar day is dear to Hari;
its merit is greater than all sacred places, truly, truly, without a doubt.”

are freed from sins such as slaying a brahmin; there is no doubt about this.
There is nothing equal to Viṣṇu’s dharma, nor to the meaning of the Gītā, O best of kings;
there is no vow equal to Ekādaśī that destroys sins.”

6.42.m:
Black sesame seeds are commended for bathing and eating, O sage;
one should present them diligently according to one’s ability to an excellent Brahmin.
As many seeds as sprout from the sesame, O Brahmin, on the earth,

The venerable Kṛṣṇa said:
Not even one [of them] there spoke of the Ṣaṭtilā Ekādaśī vow;
then by another it was said there: “The mortal woman must be seen by me.”

is freed easily from all sins.
And a gift given properly to a worthy recipient destroys all sins;
there is no misfortune, nor torment in the body, O best of kings.

6.43.m:
The venerable Kṛṣṇa said:
I shall tell, O lord of kings, of the one that occurs in the bright fortnight of Māgha;
it is renowned by the name Jayā, the supreme destroyer of all sins,
